{
  // Named after Cimoooooooo (Cimo), the Yu-Gi-Oh! content creator most strongly
  // associated with Sky Striker — his channel hosts "An Introduction to Sky
  // Strikers!" and "How to Beat Sky Strikers!", and he Top-cut/won events with
  // Trickstar Sky Striker (Dragon Duel NAWCQ 2018 1st place). See "sources".
  //
  // Pure "spell-grind control" Sky Striker. Every card name verified verbatim
  // against vendor/BabelCDB/cards.cdb via `node bin/ygo.js card`. NOTE: the
  // exact card name is "Sky Striker Ace = Zero" (equals sign, not a hyphen).
  "name": "Cimoooooooo Sky Striker",
  "category": "curated",
  "format": "classic",
  "manual": "GLOSSARY. **MMZ** = your five Main Monster Zones (your front row). **EMZ** = the two Extra Monster Zones, shared by both players, sitting in columns 2 and 4 between the two Main Monster Zone rows; a Link Monster there with an upward arrow points ACROSS into the opponent's Main Monster Zone. **Ace** = any \"Sky Striker Ace\" Link Monster. **The 3+ threshold** = the clause \"if you have 3 or more Spells in your GY\" that upgrades almost every Sky Striker Spell. **Tag out** = Tribute Raye, or Link an Ace away, to swap it for a different Ace.\n\n=== BIG PICTURE ===\nKeep your Main Monster Zone EMPTY and grind the opponent out with a self-refuelling spell engine. Almost every Sky Striker card has the clause \"If you control no monsters in your Main Monster Zone\", so you deliberately field nothing there, fire off Quick-Play spells across BOTH turns, and win the long game on card quality. The whole deck snowballs on one number: **Spells in your GY**. At 3+ Spells in GY every core spell 'upgrades' (Engage draws, Hornet Drones' token becomes 1500, Widow Anchor steals instead of just negating, Afterburners also pops a backrow). So your turn-1 job is really 'dump spells into the GY and pass with open interaction'. TCGplayer's advanced Sky Striker guide puts the whole trap in one line: \"Since many of your Sky Striker effects require no monsters in the Main Monster Zone, make sure you're timing things carefully.\"\n\n=== THE ENGINE (memorize this) ===\n- **`Sky Striker Mobilize - Engage!` is the heart.** Controlling no MMZ monster, it searches ANY Sky Striker card, and if you already have 3+ Spells in GY it also draws 1. It is a one-card toolbox — grab Hornet Drones for a body, Widow Anchor for interaction, Area Zero for grind, Multirole for lock, or a second Engage line. Recur it every turn with Kagari.\n- **`Sky Striker Mecha - Hornet Drones` makes your Link fodder.** Summons a Sky Striker Ace Token (DARK/Level 1/Warrior; its name counts as a 'Sky Striker Ace' monster, so it is legal material for the Ace Links). At 3+ Spells in GY the token is 1500/1500 instead of 0/0. It cannot be Tributed, and being a Token it is illegal material for `Relinquished Anima`.\n- **`Sky Striker Ace - Raye` is the free body.** Quick Effect: Tribute her to Special Summon a \"Sky Striker Ace\" monster from the Extra Deck to the EMZ — do this on the OPPONENT'S turn so your MMZ is empty on your turn. From the GY she re-summons herself when one of your Ace Links is destroyed by battle or leaves by an opponent's effect, so she grinds forever. Search her with `Reinforcement of the Army`. NOTE the Extra Deck restriction: `Azalea`, `Zeke` and `Azalea Temperance` all read \"Must (first) be Link Summoned\", so Raye can only cheat out the four Link-1 Aces **and `Sky Striker Ace = Zero`** — that missing clause on = Zero is the whole reason it is in the list.\n- **`Sky Striker Ace - Roze` (1-of extender):** Special Summons from hand when any Sky Striker Ace (including the Hornet token) is summoned, giving you a second body for a Link-2. WARNING: she lands in the MMZ, which temporarily disables the 'no MMZ monster' spells — so only bring her out to immediately Link her away.\n\n=== EXTRA DECK ===\n**HOW THE ACES CHAIN.** Every Ace Link is itself a \"Sky Striker Ace\" monster, and each Link-1 Ace only excludes its OWN Attribute (Kagari is FIRE and takes \"1 non-FIRE Sky Striker Ace monster\", and so on). So an Ace already on the field is legal material for a different-Attribute Ace. Yugipedia's archetype page: the Links \"are easily summoned on top of each other, with many requiring just one 'Sky Striker Ace' of a different Attribute.\" One Raye or one Hornet Token therefore buys you TWO Aces in a turn.\n\nA player spelling the chain out: \"**A single combo can go like Raye -> Hayate -> Kagari -> Shizuku.** Opponent removes Shizuku, special summon Raye -> Kaina/Shizuku. Link into Hayate when it is your turn again.\" Negative evidence for how central this is: Master Duel Meta lists `There Can Be Only One` as anti-Striker tech precisely because \"this **prevents you from Link Summoning a Sky Striker Link monster using another one as material**.\"\n⚠ RULES TRAP: Raye SPECIAL SUMMONS an Ace, it does not Link Summon it. Master Duel Meta: the Aces \"are **improperly Summoned if Summoned via Raye effect**\" — so an Ace you tagged into off Raye cannot later be revived from the GY, and `Azalea`, `Zeke` and `Azalea Temperance` (which say \"Must (first) be Link Summoned\") can never come off Raye at all.\n\n**THE STANDARD SEQUENCE.** Cimoooooooo, walking through his own YCS Chicago list: \"You're going to go through like **Kagari Shizuku** turn one, and then most likely **Kagari Hayate** on the following turn, and then you'll probably kill them by then.\" Kagari goes FIRST on a setup turn (it recycles the moment it lands); Shizuku goes LAST (her search is an End Phase trigger, so she has to be the body you pass turn with). Cimo's own turn-1 line, verbatim: \"we'll go immediately into Kagari, try to get back that Engage ... we'll just link off into Shizuku, I will set two cards and we will just go to the end phase.\" Konami's own Sky Striker guide names the same endboard: \"**At the end of your first turn, you want to have `Sky Striker Ace - Shizuku` on the field with 4 or 5 Set cards.**\"\nOn a turn where you ATTACK the order flips: Hayate first in Main Phase 1, swing, mill, then Kagari in Main Phase 2 to pick the milled card up. Road of the King: \"The common play with a `Sky Striker Ace - Raye` opening is to use it to Link Summon `Sky Striker Ace - Hayate`. Direct attack with Hayate, and send `Sky Striker Mobilize - Engage!` from the deck to the graveyard\" — and then Kagari is \"summoned in Main Phase 2 to retrieve Engage from the graveyard\". A player's version of the whole loop: \"**the flow for link monsters is usually to attack with Hayate to drop something useful, Kagari to pick it up, then Shizuku to lower their monsters during their turn.**\" This is also why Hayate is never a turn-1 play: the player going first has no Battle Phase.\n\n**THE DISSENTING VIEW ON KAGARI (worth knowing).** Master Duel Meta's guides — written for a format where Kagari is Limited to 1 — argue the opposite of \"Kagari every turn\": \"**Holding Kagari for when it makes the biggest splash and can resolve uncontested is an important skill to master; resolving Kagari to use 2 Engages on turn 1 often loses you the best play you can make on your 2nd or 3rd turn.**\" With 3 copies here you can afford Cimo's aggressive line, but if two Kagari are already in the GY, switch to the Master Duel discipline: \"**Do not waste your Kagari**, you only have one and you want to use it when it makes the most impact in a game-state.\"\n\n**TAG-OUT TECH (on the OPPONENT'S turn).** Raye's Tribute is a Quick Effect, so the timing you pick matters. Master Duel Meta: \"**Play around `Effect Veiler` with Raye by using Raye in BP/EP for Kagari/Shizuku**, respectively\" — tag in the Battle Phase or End Phase, after Veiler's window has passed. And Kaina is specifically an opponent's-turn card: \"Kaina is usually used as a **tagout option for Raye during your opponent's turn**, as it can stop an attack.\"\n\n**WHERE TO PUT THEM.** Kagari's single arrow is Top-Left; Shizuku's is Top-Right. Both point ACROSS the EMZ into the opponent's Main Monster Zone row — which legally OPENS that zone for their own Extra Deck summons. Aim the arrow at their MIDDLE (3rd) zone: Kagari in the RIGHT EMZ, Shizuku in the LEFT EMZ. Road of the King's placement article: \"This is to avoid opening up additional Main Monster Zones for the opponent.\" Hayate (Bottom-Left) and Kaina (Bottom-Right) point back into your own MMZ instead, which gives the opponent nothing — and is how you get a Link Summon into a Main Monster Zone at all.\n\n**RATIOS — 3 / 2 / 1, and the reason behind them.** The governing fact, which players state plainly: **a Link Monster that leaves the field goes to the GY and never comes back to the Extra Deck**, and Sky Striker has no in-archetype way to recycle Extra Deck names. A Master Duel player putting the problem in his own words: the deck \"**has no inherent ability to put the link monsters back in the extra deck**... if I use it to swap link monsters **it is depleting a resource I can't regenerate**.\" The reply he got is this list's exact ratio: \"**Make sure to run 3 Kagari and 2-3 Shizuku**, and that should be enough for most matchups if you optimize your plays.\" Master Duel Meta names the failure mode: \"**Do not waste Striker links**, it is not advisable to go into link monsters without a purpose in going into it, as **running out of link monsters can occasionally happen to newer players**.\"\nSo \"You can only Special Summon Kagari(s) once per turn\" caps USES PER TURN; the copy count is how many GRIND TURNS you buy.\n- **3 Kagari** is the standard in TCG tournament lists (100% at 3 in 2023-24; still ~72% at 3 today). Cimoooooooo, stuck at 1 by the 2019 banlist: \"**we would play three if we could**, but it's basically your most important card in your entire extra deck.\" When it WAS Limited, YGOPRODeck's Sky Striker banlist timeline records that \"mirror matches boiled down to whoever could resolve Kagari the most.\"\n- **2 Shizuku** matches both the \"2-3 Shizuku\" advice above and the 2024 TCG norm (70% of top lists at 2, 29% at 3). The case for more: Master Duel Meta calls Shizuku \"**the most useful Sky Striker Link Monster**, being your primary searcher outside of Engage!\", and \"**you usually want to end your turn on Shizuku**.\" The case for fewer is Cimo's shrinking-search-pool argument in her bullet below.\n- **1 Hayate** is the modern TCG norm (96-98% of 2025-26 top lists run exactly 1; it sat at 2 in 2023-24 and is almost never cut to zero). The sourced trade-off for spending those slots on generics instead comes from a Striker player explaining his own \"-1 Hayate -1 Zeke -1 Kaina\" cuts for `Knightmare Phoenix` and friends: \"**I've never wanted more than 1 Zeke or Kaina so I think the extra utility of those two monsters is worth playing over more Striker names.**\" That is exactly the trade this Extra Deck makes.\n- **1 Kaina** is already generous by modern standards — 46% of 2026 TCG top lists run ZERO. Master Duel Meta: \"sometimes the attack prevention of this card is just **not enough to warrant it taking an Extra Deck slot**, and some people opt to not run it.\"\nHONEST CAVEAT: Cimoooooooo himself never states 3/2/1. His hybrid Trickstar-Striker lists ran 2 Kagari / 1 Shizuku / 1 Hayate (\"this is the perfect ratio... you don't need more than this\") and his pure list ran 1 Kagari / 3 Shizuku / 3 Hayate under the banlist. The 3/2/1 here is the TCG tournament consensus, not a Cimo quote.\n\n- **`Sky Striker Ace - Kagari` (3) — Link-1, \"1 non-FIRE Sky Striker Ace monster\", arrow Top-Left.** Your first Link on almost every turn: on Special Summon it ADDS a Sky Striker Spell back from GY to hand, and it gains 100 ATK per Spell in your GY. The loop the deck is built on, per the Master Duel card guides: \"use Engage! then summon Kagari, then add back the Engage! to use it immediately afterwards.\" Mechanically a Link Monster that leaves the field does NOT return to the Extra Deck, and you may only Special Summon Kagari once per turn — so three copies is three grind turns of recursion, not three Kagari per turn. See RATIOS above, and the dissenting \"hold your Kagari\" discipline.\n- **`Sky Striker Ace - Shizuku` (2) — Link-1, \"1 non-WATER Sky Striker Ace monster\", arrow Top-Right.** The body you END your turn on. Passive: opponent's monsters lose 100 ATK/DEF per Spell in your GY. End Phase (only if she was Special Summoned that turn): add a Sky Striker Spell from Deck **with a name different from the cards in your GY**. Cimo on why she is the pass-turn Ace: \"Shizuku is a nice defensive option because it lowers the stats of all your opponent's monsters, it gets you a card for next turn, and if you have Ray[e] in the graveyard it's going to be really difficult for them to kill you, because if they get rid of this you go into Ray[e], and then if they try to get at Ray[e], Ray[e] can go into another Shizuku or Kagari — it just gets out of hand very quickly.\"\n  COUNTER-INTUITIVE: Shizuku gets WORSE as the game runs long while Kagari gets better, because her search pool shrinks every time a new Spell name hits your GY. Cimo, comparing the two: \"I think Kagari is better because it's always going to be recurring from the graveyard... **especially as the game goes on where Shizuku gets particularly weaker as the game progresses**. Shizuku is kind of like a good starter card, but after that it kind of loses its validity, because once there's more spells in the grave, Shizuku's gonna have less targets to choose from.\" Front-load her.\n- **`Sky Striker Ace - Hayate` (1) — Link-1, \"1 non-WIND Sky Striker Ace monster\", arrow Bottom-Left.** Attacks directly, and after it battles sends a Sky Striker card from Deck to GY. Cimo: \"Hayate can tremendously speed up our engine going second by attacking, sending a Sky Striker spell card to the grave, then tagging out into Kagari to add that card to our hand... **while Hayate doesn't generate any actual card advantage, it's a key component that helps the deck run much more smoothly**.\" TCGplayer's guide gives the same use: \"you might start with `Sky Striker Ace - Hayate` to put it in the graveyard where Kagari can get it.\" So Hayate is a tempo/consistency Ace, not a value Ace: it turns your Battle Phase into a Foolish Burial for the 3+ threshold and hand-feeds Kagari.\n  BONUS, per Master Duel Meta: \"Hayate is your **primary aggressor**, and she is very important as she can fill your grave with spells to reach the necessary spell count of 3... **Hayate also cannot be ash'd, as her effect triggers in damage calculation.**\" (That is their claim about Damage Step timing, quoted as-is — worth knowing, worth testing before you rely on it.)\n- **`Sky Striker Ace - Kaina` (1) — Link-1, \"1 non-EARTH Sky Striker Ace monster\", arrow Bottom-Right.** On Special Summon, one face-up opponent monster cannot attack until the end of their turn; plus 100 LP every time a Sky Striker Spell or its effect resolves. This is the emergency Ace and an OPPONENT'S-TURN card, not a default main-phase play — Master Duel Meta: \"Kaina is usually used as a **tagout option for Raye during your opponent's turn**, as it can stop an attack.\" Cimo: \"Kaina is not something you go into too often... **you could maybe get away with even just one**.\" Nicolas Ortiz, on Cimo's channel, on why a second copy is a trap: \"never in my life did I need [a second Kaina]... **If you're ever in a situation where you need to summon a second Kaina, something's wrong, you're probably losing.**\"\n- **`Sky Striker Ace - Azalea` (1) — Link-2, \"2 LIGHT and/or DARK monsters\", arrows Top-Left + Bottom-Right.** DO NOT CUT THIS AND DO NOT TREAT IT AS A REMOVAL CARD. A creator who devoted a segment of \"Sky Striker 101: HOW TO BREAK BOARDS\" to a viewer who had cut it: \"**I do want to go over why Azalea is not only not cuttable in Sky Striker, it is going to be one of the most used cards, especially if you're going to be going second.**\" His reason is the MMZ problem, not the pop: \"**a lot of the times going second, you sometimes just go into Azalea just to clear up your main monster zone.** You can use cards like Widow Anchor, taking one of your opponent's monsters, and let's just say you had to like normal summon a Roze or something, and you wanted to end up using additional spell cards — **you link both monsters off, go into your Link-2 Azalea just to clear the main monster zone**, and on top of that, Azalea has the effect...\" His summary: \"**think of Azalea as a way to clear up your main monster zone, as well as a Widow Anchor target... always try to go into Azalea going second, and do not cut this card from your extra deck.**\" 74% of Sky Striker lists run it; 85% of tournament-sourced ones.\n  MATERIALS — the one Ace that does NOT take an Ace. It cannot use Kagari/Shizuku/Hayate/Kaina (FIRE/WATER/WIND/EARTH). A Striker creator: \"**Azalea is the only one here that not only doesn't require Sky Striker Ace, she actually can't be made using any of the armors... you have to either steal a monster or have any combination of Raye, Roze and the Hornet Drones token.**\" Another: \"Azalea is very powerful. Whereas otherwise, we just don't have that many light and dark monsters within our extra deck. **So Azalea can be difficult to make**\", and it \"must be Link Summoned, so we can't summon it off of Raye\". (In this list: DARK `Raye`, the DARK Hornet Token, LIGHT `Roze`, DARK `Zeke`, or a stolen monster of the right Attribute. `= Zero` is LIGHT but \"Cannot be used as Link Material\".)\n  THE THRESHOLD, STATED PRECISELY — a lot of guides get this backwards, so use this version: \"**she will destroy herself if you don't have more than three spells in grave — not three spells, MORE than three.**\" So 4+ Spells in GY for the body to stick; below that Azalea pops a card and then feeds itself to the GY. Its second use is as \"**a sort of portal into your Sky Striker engine utilizing monsters that you otherwise wouldn't be as incentivized to play**\" — i.e. an off-Attribute body you drew becomes Link fodder. It can also banish a GY Spell at the start of the Damage Step to destroy the monster it battles.\n- **`Sky Striker Ace = Zero` (1) — Link-2, \"2 Sky Striker Ace monsters\", arrows Bottom-Left + Bottom-Right.** The Link-2 you almost never Link Summon, and the strongest card in a modern Striker Extra Deck — deck profiles call it \"our bread and butter\" and \"**the new Shizuku**\" (i.e. the body you end your turn on). It is the only Link-2-or-higher Ace WITHOUT a \"Must be Link Summoned\" clause: \"**Zero does not have to be Link Summoned, so we can go Raye, Raye effect tribute itself to summon out a Zero. Even though Zero is a Link-2, we can just summon it with one monster if that monster is Raye.**\" TCGplayer's advanced guide gives the whole line: \"Just Normal Summon [Raye] and use its effect to bring in Zero. That'll get Engage, and Engage will get a piece of interaction such as Widow Anchor. On your opponent's turn, you have Zero to bring in Raye and Roze while destroying a card.\"\n  WHICH TARGET TO GRAB (it adds from **Deck OR GY**): \"if you already have your three spell cards in your graveyard... **you would rather add your Engage from the graveyard** to your hand because you want to be able to draw Engages from your deck more frequently... It also plays around Droll a little bit.\"\n  TWO TRAPS. (1) \"You can only use 1 `Sky Striker Ace = Zero` effect per turn, and only once that turn\" — a deck profile spells out the cost: \"**if you summon out Zero and you search a spell, you cannot then tag it out for a Raye and Rose and pop. You have to pick which one you want to do.**\" (2) \"Cannot be used as Link Material\" dead-ends your Link climb; the sourced workaround is to climb through Zeke instead — \"a lot of our Link-2s cannot be used as Link Material. For example, **Zero cannot be used as Link Material.** So we're going to have to like go into Zeke, or just use four monsters straight up on our board, which is going to be pretty difficult.\"\n- **`Sky Striker Ace - Zeke` (1) — Link-2, \"2 monsters, including a Sky Striker Ace monster\", arrows Top + Bottom.** The universal 1-of: it appears in essentially every Sky Striker Extra Deck across TCG, Master Duel and Duel Links. The second material can be ANY monster — including one you just took control of, which is the entire point. Master Duel Meta's in-depth guide: \"**He is nice for cleaning up boards with more than 1 monster in the MMZ, so you can activate your Striker spells.** Zeke comes in clutch with Raye and Roze hands, and his banish effect can be used to bait out a lot of cards.\" A player on the same theme: \"when your opponent pops a Kaiju on you or you just stole a monster with Widow Anchor/Shark Cannon, **Zeke helps immensely in clearing main monster zones so that you can keep using those Sky Striker spells**.\" A YGOPRODeck list puts it as \"primarily to link away your opponent's monster you gained control over and to banish an opponent's monster on board.\"\n  THE HIDDEN COMBO (sourced): Zeke's once-per-turn \"target 1 other card you control; this card gains 1000 ATK, then send that card to the GY\" should usually target **`Sky Striker Airspace - Area Zero`**. Master Duel Meta: \"**Zeke, along with Area Zero, is useful to link climb as you can send Area Zero to the grave with Zeke, and get a free special summon using Area Zero's effect.**\" A spent Field Spell becomes +1000 ATK AND a free Sky Striker Ace out of the Deck.\n  Zeke \"Must be Link Summoned\", so Raye can never tag into it — \"keep in mind that you cannot summon Zeke off of Raye's effect.\"\n- **`Sky Striker Ace - Azalea Temperance` (1) — Link-3, \"2+ monsters, including a Link Monster\", arrows Left/Right/Bottom.** On Special Summon: banish a Spell from hand or GY, then EQUIP an opponent monster with 2500 or less ATK to itself. If it dies in battle it Special Summons another Sky Striker monster from hand/GY. The clearest statement of what it is FOR — again the Main Monster Zone, and again it is the same problem: \"**Sky Striker spells require there to be no monsters in the main monster zone. And previously, game plans could get quite convoluted while you controlled a Link-2 and took control of a monster through something like a Widow Anchor.** Maybe you could use a Multirole to remove that pesky main monster, or link into a Knightmare Unicorn just for the heck of it. Or just accept the fact that your Sky Striker spells are going to be out of commission for a little while. **But now the strategy has a Link-3 that gets much better use than Knightmare Unicorn. Being able to EQUIP a monster to this card instead of destroying it helps get around protection effects** like that of Thunder Dragon Colossus, who can protect itself from getting destroyed by card effect **but not from getting yoinked.**\"\n  THE HIDDEN COST nobody warns you about on the card: \"**you're banishing a Spell again — you want your spells in the graveyard, you want to be able to recycle them; banishing spells is a big no-no**... also if you're playing a Multirole your backrow is probably going to be clogged up already.\" Every Spell you banish for Temperance is one Kagari can never get back and one that stops counting toward the 3+ threshold. That is the real reason it stays a 1-of.\n  The dissent is loud: \"**it doesn't see a whole lot of play**... this is a Link-3 that says it must be Link Summoned, so **we cannot use Raye** to bring it out\", and, listing the deck's removal, \"it's just Zeke, and **I guess Azalea Temperance if you want to count that, which I don't**.\" Game8: \"not currently part of any meta deck in Master Duel.\" Adoption is real but niche — roughly 20% of lists, ~11% of tournament-sourced ones.\n  UNSOURCED for THIS list: the only documented assembly line uses modern support this deck does not run. Verifiable constraints only: materials must total Link 3 and include a Link Monster (e.g. Kagari + Raye + Token, or Zeke + one more body), and `= Zero` is illegal fodder.\n- **`Knightmare Phoenix` (1) — Link-2, \"2 monsters with different names\", arrows Top + Right.** Two jobs, and the card text only tells you one of them.\n  (a) IT IS YOUR IMPERIAL ORDER BUTTON. Master Duel Meta's in-depth Sky Striker guide: \"**Knightmare Phoenix is a link-2 used primarily as an out for Imperial Order, and other troublesome backrow.** It is a very useful toolbox card that has virtually no downside to being run.\" A long-time Striker player, listing what he cut to fit it: \"**Phoenix is basically the deck's only out to Imperial Order so that is 100% mandatory IMO.**\" On the discard cost: \"with the advantage generated by your Spells, **the discard cost is negligible** except in situations where your hand is weaker than your opponent's.\" (Nobody sourced says WHAT to pitch; note only that a discarded Spell still lands in your GY and counts toward the 3+ threshold.)\n  (b) IT CLEARS YOUR OWN MAIN MONSTER ZONE. Cimoooooooo, on his own Sky Striker list: \"**Phoenix is a very nice link off with something like Widow Anchor plus one of our cards to make sure our main monster zone is free**, and also popping annoying floodgates in back row is just very good.\"\n  THIS IS THE DECK'S MOST IMPORTANT NON-OBVIOUS RULE. A monster you take with `Widow Anchor`, or Special Summon with `Shark Cannon`, lands in YOUR Main Monster Zone — and instantly switches your whole deck off, because Engage, Hornet Drones, Widow Anchor, Shark Cannon, Afterburners, Jamming Waves and Hercules Base all require \"you control no monsters in your Main Monster Zone\". **Stealing is a cost.** Phoenix is how you cash the stolen body in: stolen monster + any differently-named monster of yours -> Phoenix, your MMZ is clear again, and their floodgate dies. Duel Links Meta's Striker guide names the pattern: the Knightmares are \"**the strongest of any Generic Link Monsters we could make with the monsters taken from the opponent.** Of course, at the cost of a discard, they're removal as well.\"\n  Honest context: Phoenix is a legacy / small-format slot. It was in roughly 40% of 2022 Master Duel Striker lists and is still in ~38% of current Duel Links lists, but ~0% of current TCG lists — displaced by the archetype's own newer Links. In THIS 'classic' list it is the only Spell/Trap removal in the Extra Deck.\n- **`Knightmare Unicorn` (1) — Link-3, \"2+ monsters with different names\", arrows Left/Right/Bottom.** Master Duel Meta: \"Unicorn is just a generic Link-3 that can shuffle any card on the field into the deck, instead of destroying and sending it to the GY. **Fulfilling the roles of both Phoenix and Cerberus**, it's more difficult to Summon due to being a Link-3, costing more resources\"; and \"its ability to return a card to the deck is very useful against problematic cards.\" Cimoooooooo, on his own list: \"We also have Unicorn for the same reason. **Just to deal with problematic threats that the deck can't really out naturally.**\" A YGOPRODeck list gives the same role — \"shuffle back your opponent's card\". Shuffle-into-Deck beats what `Afterburners!` and `Azalea` cannot: destruction-immune bosses, and monsters that float when destroyed.\n  Materials, per a Striker deck profile: \"**it's also the easiest one for this deck to make just because Raye is a dark and Zeke is a dark**\" — Raye + Zeke are two different names, so Zeke (Link-2) + Raye is your cheapest Link-3, and a stolen monster works as the odd body out. Same MMZ-clearing function as Phoenix, one rating higher and with a 2200 beater attached.\n- **`Salamangreat Almiraj` (1) — Link-1, \"1 Normal Summoned/Set monster with 1000 or less ATK\", arrow Bottom-Right.** A genuine fringe slot — roughly 1-4% of TCG Sky Striker lists depending how the sample is drawn, and **ZERO of 242 pure Master Duel Sky Striker top decks** — and NEITHER of Master Duel Meta's two in-depth Sky Striker guides, nor Game8's, nor any Cimoooooooo video mentions it even once. But its job IS sourced, and it is not the one on the card: **Almiraj is the emergency eject button for a monster stuck in your Main Monster Zone.** A Master Duel Sky Striker player, in the card-by-card notes on his own top-ranked list, on a Normal Summon that can get hand-trapped: \"**Just make sure you have a way to get it off the board** if you do use it in case it gets hand trapped (**For this I play Almiraj as a last resort**)\"; and on a later list, \"I would probably **drop a Hayate for Almiraj to link off** [that Normal Summon].\" An OCG Striker player describes exactly the line this list can run: \"**normal summoning handtraps, then linking it to Almiraj**.\" The generic community shorthand is \"**you use Almiraj to turn dead hand traps into a Link-1**\" / \"handtraps are pseudo extenders via Almiraj\".\n  MATERIAL CHECK for THIS list: `Raye` and `Roze` are 1500 ATK and can NEVER make it; the Hornet Token is Special Summoned so it does not qualify either. **The only legal materials you own are a Normal-Summoned `Ash Blossom`, `Effect Veiler` or `Ghost Belle` (all 0 ATK)** — so Almiraj exists to convert a hand trap you were never going to use into a body, and to get it out of your Main Monster Zone. It is also the only non-Ace Link-1 you own, i.e. a Link Monster body for `Azalea Temperance`'s \"including a Link Monster\" clause.\n  ⚠ THREE HONESTY FLAGS, and they matter more than the paragraph above. (1) Every human source stops at \"clear the Main Monster Zone\" / \"get it off the board\" — **nobody actually states the further step \"…so your Sky Striker Spells turn back on.\"** The inference is obvious, but it is not attested. (2) Do NOT plan around its Tribute-protection effect; no source ties it to any Sky Striker Ace, and a player on that effect generally: \"the protection effect kind of doesn't come into play often, **especially since you don't usually keep Almiraj on the field**.\" (3) THE CONTRARY EVIDENCE IS STRONG. Every VIDEO source that puts Almiraj in a Sky Striker deck is **Invoked** Sky Striker, and every stated reason is an Invoked reason (Aleister the Invoker is 1000 ATK, so Almiraj dumps him to fuel Invocation recursion, and the tribute protects Mechaba) — not a Sky Striker reason. A Mar 2026 Striker creator raises EXACTLY the problem this card would solve and says the deck had no answer to it: \"**before, we would have nothing that we can do with monsters in the main monster zone.** However, with Camellia, you could like normal summon a hand trap afterwards and then go into Camellia and get access to engine.\" And what pure Striker historically did with a dead Normal-Summonable hand trap was Halqifibrax -> Selene -> Accesscode, not Almiraj. Across ~290 Sky Striker videos, 465 decklists, and both wikis, **no list anywhere runs both Almiraj and `Relinquished Anima`**, and 0 of 242 pure Master Duel Sky Striker top decks run either. In every human source, the Main-Monster-Zone job is done by `Knightmare Phoenix`, `Knightmare Unicorn`, `Sky Striker Ace - Zeke` or `Sky Striker Ace - Azalea` — all of which you already have. Treat Almiraj as the fifth option, not the first.\n- **`Relinquished Anima` (1) — Link-1, \"1 Level 1 monster, except a Token\", single arrow Top.** EXACTLY ONE human is on record running this in a pure Sky Striker list, and his verdict is negative — read it before you plan around the card. Squiddys, reading out his F2P Master Duel Striker Extra Deck: \"**one Anima — this is just a random card, because you can go in with Veiler**... it being a Link Monster makes it easier to go into Ningirsu as well.\" In his next build: \"**one Relinquished — this card like never comes up, so you probably just cut it for a [Knightmare] Unicorn. I might just do that.**\" That independently confirms the material analysis below (Effect Veiler) and tells you the honest expected value. Inclusion data agrees: **0 of 462 TCG Sky Striker tournament top decks and 3 of 1731 Master Duel ones.** The only other Striker-specific note found anywhere is a YGOPRODeck primer saying it \"turns a Level 1 into a Spellcaster for Selene\" — a `Selene, Queen of the Master Magicians` package this list does not play. Treat the slot as unjustified until you find better.\n  ⚠ A COST OF THE ARROW: because Anima's only marker points UP at the opponent, it opens NO zone of your own. A creator on that: \"**Relinquished Anima actually points up — that can actually hurt, because you're not going to be able to Link Summon beneath Relinquished Anima**... you actually have to link off the Relinquished Anima or find some way to get this off of the table.\" (Said about a different deck, but it is a property of the card, not the deck.)\n  How the CARD works is well sourced. Its one upward arrow is the whole card. Road of the King's placement article, describing it as a threat to guard against: \"when the opponent's `Relinquished Anima` is summoned in the left Extra Monster Zone it would be able to target the monster in your 2nd Main Monster Zone, and when summoned in the right Extra Monster Zone it would be able to target the monster in your 4th Main Monster Zone\" — i.e. from an EMZ it points ACROSS at whatever sits in the opponent's Main Monster Zone in that same column, and equips it (permanent, non-destruction, non-targeting-by-destruction, and gone from their field). The Judgment of the Pharaoh review gives both the limit and the second line: \"Due its arrow's position is obvious that 'Anima' tries to go towards and against the Extra Monster Zone... **Can only target monsters located inside the Extra Monster Zones or its columns**\", and notes you can also be \"**taking advantage of an enemy's arrow to be Link Summoned**\" — that is, if the opponent's EMZ monster points down into your Main Monster Zone, Link Summon Anima into THAT zone and its up-arrow points straight back at them. The same review calls it \"far from a staple option in most Extra Decks.\"\n  MATERIAL CHECK for THIS list: the only Level 1 monster you own is `Effect Veiler`; the Sky Striker Ace Token is explicitly excluded by name. The alternative is a Level 1 monster you took with `Widow Anchor` or `Shark Cannon`.\n\n=== INTERACTION SPELLS (play them on the OPPONENT'S turn) ===\n- **`Sky Striker Mecha - Widow Anchor`** — your best answer: negate a face-up Effect Monster's effects until end of turn, and at 3+ Spells in GY also take control of it until the End Phase. Chain it to a summon to cut a combo off. Remember the Phoenix rule above: the stolen monster sits in YOUR MMZ and turns your engine off until you Link it away (`Zeke`, `Knightmare Phoenix`, `Knightmare Unicorn`).\n- **`Sky Striker Maneuver - Afterburners!`** — target-destroy a face-up monster (+1 Spell/Trap at 3+ Spells). `Jamming Waves!` is the mirror: pop a Set S/T (+ a monster at 3+ Spells).\n- **`Sky Striker Mecha - Shark Cannon`** — banish a monster from their GY, or at 3+ Spells Special Summon it to your field instead (it cannot attack). Same warning: it lands in your MMZ.\n\n=== GRIND / LOCK PIECES ===\n- **`Sky Striker Mecha Modules - Multirole`:** once/turn send another card you control to GY, and your opponent CANNOT respond to your Spell activations for the rest of the turn — this pushes your key spells through Ash/interaction. Its End-Phase effect re-SETS Sky Striker Spells with different names from your GY, up to the number you activated while it was face-up (they banish when they leave the field). Cimo's caveat on it: \"this is yet another way the deck is able to out resource our opponents by doubling up on spell uses, **but it can also contradict the desire to have three or more spells in grave** and enabling the extra ability of our Sky Striker spells. A very powerful card, but requires careful planning.\"\n- **`Sky Striker Airspace - Area Zero`:** target a card you control, excavate 3, add 1 excavated Sky Striker card, and if you excavated one, send the targeted card to the GY (thins + feeds the GY count); if this card in the Field Zone is sent to the GY by a card effect it Special Summons a Sky Striker Ace from Deck. Fetch it with `Terraforming`.\n- **`Sky Striker Mecharmory - Hercules Base`:** equip an Ace so it makes a second attack on monsters (it cannot attack directly while equipped) and draws when it destroys a monster at 3+ Spells; if the equip is sent from the field to the GY by a card effect, shuffle up to 3 Sky Striker cards from your GY back into the Deck. That recycling is why it became a staple when Kagari was Limited.\n- **`Foolish Burial Goods`** dumps a Sky Striker Spell to instantly turn on all the '3+ Spells in GY' clauses. Cimo on the interaction: \"with Kagari that's why you'd want to dump something like Engage with a Foolish Burial Goods to immediately add it to hand.\"\n\n=== GOING FIRST / GOING SECOND ===\n**Going 1st:** DON'T over-commit. Fire Engage, get Spells into the GY, run the Kagari -> Shizuku chain, SET Impermanence/Solemn, and pass with Widow Anchor and hand traps up. Cimo's own turn-1 board is exactly this: Kagari for the Engage, link into Shizuku, \"set two cards and we will just go to the end phase\". You want to interact on their turn, not build a fragile board — and remember you have no Battle Phase, so Hayate is off the table.\n**Going 2nd:** you're the control deck that loves it. Ash/Veiler/Impermanence their combo, Widow Anchor the survivor, Afterburners the rest, then rebuild your empty field. This is Hayate's turn: swing directly, mill a Sky Striker card, then Kagari in Main Phase 2 to add it. Knightmare Phoenix / Unicorn are here to clear stolen bodies out of your MMZ and to answer floodgates.\n\n=== MIRROR MATCH NOTE ===\nThe whole engine funnels through the Link-1 Aces, which makes zone- and summon-denial brutal. Nicolas Ortiz, on Cimo's channel, on why `There Can Be Only One` is mained in the mirror: \"if they make Hayate, attack, and you flip this, **they can't go Kagari, they can't go Shizuku** — slows down their turn and tempo.\" Assume your opponent will try the same thing to you: do not commit an Ace you cannot afford to have stranded.\n\n=== WEAKNESSES ===\n1. The deck folds to being FORCED to have a monster in the MMZ — an opponent's mandatory Special Summon into your zone, your own Roze left stranded, or your own Widow Anchor/Shark Cannon theft left un-Linked. Everything shuts off.\n2. Spell/Trap floodgates and anti-Special hate (Imperial Order, Anti-Spell Fragrance) hose the engine.\n3. It is low-damage: without Hercules Base / Hayate it can durdle and get milled or decked out.\n4. The 3+ Spells-in-GY threshold means your FIRST turn is weakest; GY hate that banishes your GY spells resets that clock and cripples Kagari recursion.\n5. Your Aces' upward arrows hand the opponent an extra Main Monster Zone — see WHERE TO PUT THEM above.",
